WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.480
[MUSIC PLAYING]

00:00:06.450 --> 00:00:08.760
BARDIA DEJBAN: Thank you
for joining this session

00:00:08.760 --> 00:00:12.210
where I get to be the messenger
to the team's great work

00:00:12.210 --> 00:00:14.520
in accomplishing an
amazing migration.

00:00:14.520 --> 00:00:16.560
Actually, there's
been a single thread

00:00:16.560 --> 00:00:19.680
that's been common among all the
leaders and the decision-makers

00:00:19.680 --> 00:00:22.260
that I've been talking
with in the past week.

00:00:22.260 --> 00:00:25.260
And that is that there's
always one thing--

00:00:25.260 --> 00:00:28.980
one thing that leads up to the
decision and one thing that

00:00:28.980 --> 00:00:31.770
leads up to the catalyst--

00:00:31.770 --> 00:00:34.770
to be able to actually make
the decision to move to cloud.

00:00:34.770 --> 00:00:38.250
And for us, this was it.

00:00:38.250 --> 00:00:40.350
It was a moment of clarity
when our head of IT

00:00:40.350 --> 00:00:41.640
delivered this message to me.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.152
And, funny enough, my
five-year-old son at home,

00:00:45.152 --> 00:00:46.610
he came to me and
he saw the slide.

00:00:46.610 --> 00:00:49.459
He said, oh my gosh,
Daddy, what happened?

00:00:49.459 --> 00:00:51.000
And I said, don't
worry, don't worry,

00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:52.583
Daddy is not going
to let that happen.

00:00:55.840 --> 00:00:57.600
So what are we
going to talk about?

00:00:57.600 --> 00:00:59.400
We're going to talk
about all the decisions

00:00:59.400 --> 00:01:01.740
that led up to us
migrating the cloud

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:04.050
and why we decided
on Google Cloud.

00:01:04.050 --> 00:01:06.450
We're going to talk
about our initial plans.

00:01:06.450 --> 00:01:07.200
And you know what?

00:01:07.200 --> 00:01:08.658
Things changed, so
we're also going

00:01:08.658 --> 00:01:12.840
to talk about what
actually really happened.

00:01:12.840 --> 00:01:16.120
Just a reminder for the folks
that don't know the Volusion.

00:01:16.120 --> 00:01:18.500
We are all-in-one
commerce platform--

00:01:18.500 --> 00:01:19.814
we're a SaaS platform.

00:01:19.814 --> 00:01:21.480
And we are the only
ones in the industry

00:01:21.480 --> 00:01:23.850
that are focused on SMBs.

00:01:23.850 --> 00:01:25.650
And the Small Business
Administration

00:01:25.650 --> 00:01:28.240
defines SMBs in
many different ways.

00:01:28.240 --> 00:01:31.530
And the one fact, or the
metric-- that stands out to me,

00:01:31.530 --> 00:01:33.510
is the fact that
SMBs make, generally,

00:01:33.510 --> 00:01:37.770
less than $50 million annually--
those are our customers.

00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.820
At the heart of everything
that we do is our culture code.

00:01:41.820 --> 00:01:44.710
And, in fact, we reinvented
it a couple of years ago.

00:01:44.710 --> 00:01:47.460
And one of the most important
slides in our culture code

00:01:47.460 --> 00:01:48.960
that drives every
decision we make

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.940
is that question, what's
in it for the customer?

00:01:51.940 --> 00:01:54.300
And if you saw PH's
session yesterday

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:57.550
about being the customer, that's
what we're talking about here.

00:01:57.550 --> 00:01:59.970
And if you wanted to check
out more on our culture code,

00:01:59.970 --> 00:02:01.884
please go to
culture.volusion.com.

00:02:01.884 --> 00:02:03.695
Hopefully, it
influences you like

00:02:03.695 --> 00:02:05.820
we were influenced to create
it in the first place.

00:02:08.479 --> 00:02:10.039
Just a point of clarity--

00:02:10.039 --> 00:02:12.720
we actually have two
offerings for Volusion.

00:02:12.720 --> 00:02:16.550
We have what we refer to
internally as V1 and V2.

00:02:16.550 --> 00:02:18.410
So V2 is modern in the cloud.

00:02:18.410 --> 00:02:20.050
It's our new offering.

00:02:20.050 --> 00:02:22.050
But for the purposes
of this session,

00:02:22.050 --> 00:02:24.830
we're going to focus on V1.

00:02:24.830 --> 00:02:26.900
What is V1?

00:02:26.900 --> 00:02:31.380
It's software that's been pretty
much in development since 1999.

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:33.440
It's got a lot of code.

00:02:33.440 --> 00:02:38.150
It's predominantly Windows,
IIS, Microsoft SQL Server.

00:02:38.150 --> 00:02:39.710
And the team, over
the years, has

00:02:39.710 --> 00:02:42.530
figured out how to
orchestrate and how

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.780
to put customers on
different segments

00:02:44.780 --> 00:02:47.967
and different groupings
of IIS and database

00:02:47.967 --> 00:02:49.550
depending on the
volume that they get.

00:02:49.550 --> 00:02:52.070
So some merchants get
more traffic than others.

00:02:52.070 --> 00:02:56.360
And the isolation, the teams
figured out over the years.

00:02:56.360 --> 00:02:59.990
As an example, we actually
have pretty huge traffic spikes

00:02:59.990 --> 00:03:01.130
for certain customers.

00:03:01.130 --> 00:03:03.680
In fact, over a two-
or three-day period,

00:03:03.680 --> 00:03:06.800
we experience very heavy
loads, and we call that period

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.430
Black Friday where our merchants
get a lot of shopper traffic.

00:03:13.710 --> 00:03:18.950
So let's talk about why
cloud and why Google Cloud?

00:03:18.950 --> 00:03:20.112
So here's a picture--

00:03:20.112 --> 00:03:21.320
actually, this is misleading.

00:03:21.320 --> 00:03:25.100
We had Data Center 0.1, which
is the founder's bedroom closet.

00:03:25.100 --> 00:03:27.170
This is Volusion
Data Center 1.0.

00:03:27.170 --> 00:03:28.940
And one thing that
you're going to notice

00:03:28.940 --> 00:03:30.440
is single points of failure--

00:03:30.440 --> 00:03:34.350
single CPU, single
network, single disk.

00:03:34.350 --> 00:03:38.060
And this is actually a
closet in one of the offices.

00:03:38.060 --> 00:03:40.700
But we evolved, we
learned, we got better.

00:03:40.700 --> 00:03:45.260
And we went to a colocation
in Simi Valley, California.

00:03:45.260 --> 00:03:47.930
But you'll still see
the same problems--

00:03:47.930 --> 00:03:50.990
single points of failure,
single deadbolt security

00:03:50.990 --> 00:03:54.200
to get in, single
network, single power.

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:58.040
So the team invested
in a tier 4 colocation,

00:03:58.040 --> 00:04:01.070
and that's based in Austin,
Texas, and it's phenomenal.

00:04:01.070 --> 00:04:03.170
The security is amazing.

00:04:03.170 --> 00:04:07.310
The redundancy on power
and cooling is amazing.

00:04:07.310 --> 00:04:11.600
And, in fact, the initial
proposal from the team

00:04:11.600 --> 00:04:14.030
was, let's move everything
from Simi Valley, California

00:04:14.030 --> 00:04:15.957
to this tier 4 colocation.

00:04:15.957 --> 00:04:17.540
And what that would
mean is, sometimes

00:04:17.540 --> 00:04:19.910
you have to buy a
bunch of hardware

00:04:19.910 --> 00:04:22.940
so that you can move over the
customers, and most of the apps

00:04:22.940 --> 00:04:24.357
can come over,
but you might also

00:04:24.357 --> 00:04:26.190
have to acquire some
software along the way.

00:04:29.422 --> 00:04:30.880
And then I had to
ask myself, which

00:04:30.880 --> 00:04:32.980
is a question that
some of you here

00:04:32.980 --> 00:04:35.860
might be asking yourselves,
which is should we

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:37.420
be in the data center business?

00:04:37.420 --> 00:04:39.190
Or what value
would our customers

00:04:39.190 --> 00:04:43.510
get from us being in the
data center business?

00:04:43.510 --> 00:04:44.890
So we dug into the facts.

00:04:44.890 --> 00:04:48.430
And I found out something really
interesting-- that over 50%

00:04:48.430 --> 00:04:51.640
of our failures were
the result of hardware

00:04:51.640 --> 00:04:54.850
or the interconnectivity between
that hardware within the data

00:04:54.850 --> 00:04:58.400
center-- we call that network.

00:04:58.400 --> 00:05:00.890
So we started to go to
all the calculators on all

00:05:00.890 --> 00:05:04.160
the major cloud platforms,
and we started to scope out,

00:05:04.160 --> 00:05:05.370
what does it look like?

00:05:05.370 --> 00:05:08.840
How much is it going to cost for
us to move over to the cloud?

00:05:08.840 --> 00:05:12.260
And initial savings could
have been somewhere around 20%

00:05:12.260 --> 00:05:15.230
to 21%, so that sounded good.

00:05:15.230 --> 00:05:18.740
That's money that we can use
for R&amp;D for our customers.

00:05:18.740 --> 00:05:21.350
We could repurpose that.

00:05:21.350 --> 00:05:23.870
But how do you know which
cloud to use, right?

00:05:23.870 --> 00:05:25.290
There are so many choices.

00:05:25.290 --> 00:05:27.790
There's the big three,
and there are tons more.

00:05:27.790 --> 00:05:29.540
So it's really hard
to make that decision.

00:05:29.540 --> 00:05:33.500
How do you choose which
one's right for you?

00:05:33.500 --> 00:05:35.420
Everyone has a
selection process.

00:05:35.420 --> 00:05:38.210
Every company has their own
mechanisms and frameworks.

00:05:38.210 --> 00:05:39.986
For us, it was really simple.

00:05:39.986 --> 00:05:41.360
We would get a
bunch of proposals

00:05:41.360 --> 00:05:44.300
from all the vendors and
all the cloud providers.

00:05:44.300 --> 00:05:47.614
We would spend some
time evaluating.

00:05:47.614 --> 00:05:49.280
We would do a proof
of concept, perhaps,

00:05:49.280 --> 00:05:50.534
during that evaluation.

00:05:50.534 --> 00:05:51.950
And then we would
make a decision.

00:05:55.600 --> 00:05:57.590
And what happened
was phenomenal.

00:05:57.590 --> 00:06:00.130
The best conversations
that we had

00:06:00.130 --> 00:06:02.270
were with the Google Cloud team.

00:06:02.270 --> 00:06:03.980
In fact, I'll give
you an example.

00:06:03.980 --> 00:06:06.160
We had a major
networking constraint

00:06:06.160 --> 00:06:07.870
that we had to discuss.

00:06:07.870 --> 00:06:10.630
And in 24 hours,
we, as a team, got

00:06:10.630 --> 00:06:13.240
on a "Hangout" with the
team at Google Cloud

00:06:13.240 --> 00:06:15.550
that built the infrastructure--

00:06:15.550 --> 00:06:18.250
the networking infrastructure
on Google Cloud.

00:06:18.250 --> 00:06:21.040
And they were able to answer
our questions directly.

00:06:21.040 --> 00:06:24.080
And as another reference, one
of the other cloud providers,

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:26.920
not to be named, sent
us to a vendor who

00:06:26.920 --> 00:06:28.264
got back to us in two weeks.

00:06:28.264 --> 00:06:29.680
It took two weeks
just to get back

00:06:29.680 --> 00:06:31.560
to us to have a phone call.

00:06:34.120 --> 00:06:36.521
So now we're kind of
settling on Google Cloud.

00:06:36.521 --> 00:06:38.020
What's our initial
plan going to be?

00:06:38.020 --> 00:06:39.670
How do we actually do that?

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:42.700
It's starting to sound
like it could fit.

00:06:42.700 --> 00:06:45.340
Once again, that all-important
question that we ask,

00:06:45.340 --> 00:06:49.100
what's in it for the customer?

00:06:49.100 --> 00:06:52.790
And uptime is, perhaps, one
of the most important things.

00:06:52.790 --> 00:06:53.990
Our customers are SMBs.

00:06:53.990 --> 00:06:56.630
These are Small
Medium Businesses,

00:06:56.630 --> 00:06:59.720
these are moms-and-pops,
and if we go down,

00:06:59.720 --> 00:07:02.060
they can't make money
to pay their mortgage,

00:07:02.060 --> 00:07:03.380
their employees.

00:07:03.380 --> 00:07:05.070
Sometimes this is
their only business

00:07:05.070 --> 00:07:08.360
is an online e-commerce store.

00:07:08.360 --> 00:07:11.300
So if we can eliminate
hardware failures in that graph

00:07:11.300 --> 00:07:15.890
I showed you, we'd get closer
to even more nines than 99.9.

00:07:15.890 --> 00:07:17.420
And we can scale
on-demand, so we

00:07:17.420 --> 00:07:20.240
can deal with that traffic that
spikes during Black Friday.

00:07:23.030 --> 00:07:24.950
Having everybody on a
homogeneous development

00:07:24.950 --> 00:07:26.000
environment--

00:07:26.000 --> 00:07:29.630
IT, engineering, the data
teams, the ops teams--

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:30.890
would be powerful.

00:07:30.890 --> 00:07:33.140
And we'd be able to speed
up development times, having

00:07:33.140 --> 00:07:34.832
access to the same tools.

00:07:34.832 --> 00:07:36.290
And this would
benefit the customer

00:07:36.290 --> 00:07:38.690
because we'd get products
shipped to them a lot faster.

00:07:41.380 --> 00:07:45.180
Speaking of faster, some of
the case studies on Google,

00:07:45.180 --> 00:07:47.800
Google Cloud actually
showed us that people

00:07:47.800 --> 00:07:50.470
are benefiting from the
globally available network

00:07:50.470 --> 00:07:52.030
that Google has.

00:07:52.030 --> 00:07:53.481
And we felt we could be faster.

00:07:53.481 --> 00:07:55.480
The network could be
faster just by moving over.

00:08:00.500 --> 00:08:01.777
There so many other benefits.

00:08:01.777 --> 00:08:03.360
I'm sure you've read
about them, heard

00:08:03.360 --> 00:08:06.720
about them, in this Next and
last year's Next, and all

00:08:06.720 --> 00:08:08.070
the collateral.

00:08:08.070 --> 00:08:09.810
The one thing that
stood out to us

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:13.420
was the access that we would
get to the Google Cloud Platform

00:08:13.420 --> 00:08:13.920
teams.

00:08:18.720 --> 00:08:20.880
And, by the way, when
we started diving

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.420
a little bit deeper with the
Google Cloud Platform team,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:27.420
we started to see a little bit
better cost-to-serve reduction.

00:08:27.420 --> 00:08:29.520
So the estimates
were about 33% now

00:08:29.520 --> 00:08:30.940
that we would save
on monthly cost

00:08:30.940 --> 00:08:36.940
to serve that we
can reinvest in R&amp;D.

00:08:36.940 --> 00:08:39.839
So which migration
approach is right for us?

00:08:39.839 --> 00:08:41.130
There's so many different ones.

00:08:41.130 --> 00:08:42.679
These are the three
core ones that we

00:08:42.679 --> 00:08:45.620
were kind of dabbling in
and making a decision on.

00:08:45.620 --> 00:08:48.690
And on the far left, there's
the cloud-native approach,

00:08:48.690 --> 00:08:51.410
which is a complete rewrite
to match Google Cloud Platform

00:08:51.410 --> 00:08:53.290
tools and technology.

00:08:53.290 --> 00:08:56.180
All the way on the right,
it's a little bit less work,

00:08:56.180 --> 00:08:57.710
but similar.

00:08:57.710 --> 00:09:00.500
You leverage the
cloud-native aspects.

00:09:00.500 --> 00:09:01.970
Both of those that
I just mentioned

00:09:01.970 --> 00:09:03.844
are pretty high-effort
and high-cost upfront,

00:09:03.844 --> 00:09:05.450
but the savings are huge.

00:09:05.450 --> 00:09:08.330
And in the middle, there's
this thing called Lift &amp; Shift.

00:09:08.330 --> 00:09:10.310
And Life &amp; Shift
just means move over

00:09:10.310 --> 00:09:16.070
as much as you can with
minimal modification.

00:09:16.070 --> 00:09:18.047
And that's what we
decided to choose.

00:09:18.047 --> 00:09:19.130
We went with Life &amp; Shift.

00:09:19.130 --> 00:09:21.421
And what that meant is, if
we had an app server sitting

00:09:21.421 --> 00:09:23.810
in our colocation in our
data center somewhere,

00:09:23.810 --> 00:09:27.560
we would spin up an exact
instance like that in GCP

00:09:27.560 --> 00:09:28.490
and move it over.

00:09:28.490 --> 00:09:31.867
And we would only write code and
make changes if it didn't work,

00:09:31.867 --> 00:09:32.950
if there were impediments.

00:09:36.336 --> 00:09:36.960
So that's cool.

00:09:36.960 --> 00:09:39.000
We're going to do Lift
&amp; Shift, but how do

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:39.900
you actually do that?

00:09:43.940 --> 00:09:46.230
So other people say it,
usually, better than I can

00:09:46.230 --> 00:09:49.250
and, in this case, Benjamin
Franklin said it best,

00:09:49.250 --> 00:09:50.630
"Tell me and I forget.

00:09:50.630 --> 00:09:51.710
Teach me and I remember.

00:09:51.710 --> 00:09:53.360
And involve me, and I learn."

00:09:53.360 --> 00:09:55.760
And that last sentence is
what's really important to us

00:09:55.760 --> 00:09:56.840
as a company.

00:09:56.840 --> 00:09:58.970
When you involve the
teams, when you get buy-in,

00:09:58.970 --> 00:10:02.540
when you get that
learning, then everybody

00:10:02.540 --> 00:10:06.260
is actually going to help move
this thing forward quicker.

00:10:06.260 --> 00:10:08.750
And, in fact, the way that
we decided to learn upfront

00:10:08.750 --> 00:10:10.970
was to engage the experts.

00:10:10.970 --> 00:10:16.620
And at the time that we decided
to do this, we engaged Google.

00:10:16.620 --> 00:10:19.160
They had a program called
Google Cloud Start.

00:10:19.160 --> 00:10:21.260
And they brought
in their experts

00:10:21.260 --> 00:10:23.570
to do infrastructure and
architecture planning

00:10:23.570 --> 00:10:26.490
and show us, this
is how you do it.

00:10:26.490 --> 00:10:29.670
And we got to learn from them.

00:10:29.670 --> 00:10:32.120
And speaking of learning,
there were a lot of ways

00:10:32.120 --> 00:10:34.900
that we could decide to
learn and choose to learn,

00:10:34.900 --> 00:10:37.890
and we took advantage of every
single one of these ways.

00:10:37.890 --> 00:10:39.950
So we engaged
professional services--

00:10:39.950 --> 00:10:42.930
once again, the experts
that have done this before.

00:10:42.930 --> 00:10:45.442
We had workshops on-site
and virtual for the teams.

00:10:45.442 --> 00:10:47.150
We had on-site training,
and sometimes we

00:10:47.150 --> 00:10:51.290
had classes of 25 or more
people, Volusioneers,

00:10:51.290 --> 00:10:52.820
in those training
sessions learning

00:10:52.820 --> 00:10:57.350
about different technologies
that GCP has to offer.

00:10:57.350 --> 00:10:59.220
And at the time
that we did this,

00:10:59.220 --> 00:11:01.340
there were no case studies.

00:11:01.340 --> 00:11:03.590
There were no other examples
of any other companies

00:11:03.590 --> 00:11:05.030
that have done this before.

00:11:05.030 --> 00:11:09.340
So we were pioneering a way
for a SaaS platform, SaaS

00:11:09.340 --> 00:11:12.729
e-commerce company, to move
over all these customers

00:11:12.729 --> 00:11:13.645
with minimal downtime.

00:11:17.220 --> 00:11:20.030
And we had a pretty accelerated
timeline to do that.

00:11:20.030 --> 00:11:23.600
We spent about one quarter
making the decision

00:11:23.600 --> 00:11:25.730
and planning.

00:11:25.730 --> 00:11:27.800
We spent another
quarter actually

00:11:27.800 --> 00:11:30.290
doing the migration itself.

00:11:30.290 --> 00:11:33.740
We spent a couple of months
flipping over the bits

00:11:33.740 --> 00:11:36.215
to get the customers live.

00:11:36.215 --> 00:11:38.434
And then, now
we're in this phase

00:11:38.434 --> 00:11:39.850
where we call
modernization, where

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:44.610
we're trying to take advantage
of cloud-native technologies.

00:11:44.610 --> 00:11:46.880
And I find it remarkable
that last year, we

00:11:46.880 --> 00:11:49.550
were attendees to
this conference,

00:11:49.550 --> 00:11:51.989
and this year we're talking
about how we actually did it.

00:11:51.989 --> 00:11:53.780
That's a fantastic
achievement by the team.

00:11:56.520 --> 00:11:57.470
So that was the plan.

00:11:57.470 --> 00:11:58.845
So what actually
really happened?

00:12:02.580 --> 00:12:06.420
Problem number one-- because we
host thousands of websites that

00:12:06.420 --> 00:12:10.110
need certificates,
there was no technology

00:12:10.110 --> 00:12:13.140
that Google had,
natively, cloud native,

00:12:13.140 --> 00:12:15.180
to be able to host
and do SSL termination

00:12:15.180 --> 00:12:16.691
on those certificates.

00:12:16.691 --> 00:12:17.940
So this was the first problem.

00:12:17.940 --> 00:12:21.920
And we have over 50,000 of them.

00:12:21.920 --> 00:12:25.280
And the proposal that we had
approached during the Cloud

00:12:25.280 --> 00:12:28.760
Start phase was a hybrid
traffic layer using NGINX.

00:12:28.760 --> 00:12:30.560
And if you don't
know what NGINX is,

00:12:30.560 --> 00:12:33.800
it's a reverse proxy
layer, an HTTP layer,

00:12:33.800 --> 00:12:34.920
that you can add in.

00:12:34.920 --> 00:12:39.054
So we would still leverage
some of the cloud Load Balancer

00:12:39.054 --> 00:12:40.970
aspects of Google, but
we had built this layer

00:12:40.970 --> 00:12:42.706
in between called NGINX.

00:12:42.706 --> 00:12:45.080
And that would handle the SSL
hosting and the termination

00:12:45.080 --> 00:12:48.610
across all the other layers
all the way to the store

00:12:48.610 --> 00:12:49.528
resolution.

00:12:52.400 --> 00:12:54.800
I think we had to
rebuild this a few times,

00:12:54.800 --> 00:12:56.970
but eventually, this
is what we settled on.

00:12:56.970 --> 00:13:01.250
And we internally refer to
this as the three-layer cake.

00:13:01.250 --> 00:13:05.210
It's a combination of
Google Cloud at the top,

00:13:05.210 --> 00:13:07.130
with Google Cloud
Load Balancers,

00:13:07.130 --> 00:13:10.200
and NGINX spread
through the middle.

00:13:10.200 --> 00:13:13.220
What's fantastic about this
is that different teams,

00:13:13.220 --> 00:13:16.260
across the company, can focus
on their respective layers.

00:13:16.260 --> 00:13:19.760
For example, InfoSec can
focus on the security layer.

00:13:19.760 --> 00:13:21.770
And because it's
abstracted out this way,

00:13:21.770 --> 00:13:23.960
they usually don't
impact the other layers.

00:13:23.960 --> 00:13:26.930
The app engineering team can
focus on the bottommost layer.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:32.324
Problem number two.

00:13:32.324 --> 00:13:34.490
At the time that we were
going to do this migration,

00:13:34.490 --> 00:13:37.150
we had two DNS servers.

00:13:37.150 --> 00:13:39.410
And we knew that that was
going to be a bottleneck.

00:13:43.730 --> 00:13:46.640
And the solution here
was Google Cloud DNS.

00:13:46.640 --> 00:13:49.040
And, in fact, to our
surprise, the day

00:13:49.040 --> 00:13:52.970
that we cut over our
DNS to Google Cloud DNS,

00:13:52.970 --> 00:13:56.540
we saw a 15% improvement in
first-byte response time,

00:13:56.540 --> 00:13:59.460
on average, across all
of our customer sites.

00:13:59.460 --> 00:14:00.670
So that was a fantastic win.

00:14:03.730 --> 00:14:05.620
Problem number
three-- once again,

00:14:05.620 --> 00:14:08.230
because we are hosting
all these websites,

00:14:08.230 --> 00:14:10.460
there are thousands
of small files--

00:14:10.460 --> 00:14:13.600
CSS, JavaScript,
images, flat files,

00:14:13.600 --> 00:14:15.487
HTML files-- plus the database.

00:14:15.487 --> 00:14:17.695
How do you move all those
over with minimal downtime?

00:14:21.490 --> 00:14:24.220
We actually leveraged a
combination of CloudEndure

00:14:24.220 --> 00:14:26.050
and our internal scripts.

00:14:26.050 --> 00:14:29.750
And CloudEndure has a product
called Live Migration, where

00:14:29.750 --> 00:14:33.860
it'll constantly do continuous
replication from our data

00:14:33.860 --> 00:14:36.010
center over to Google Cloud.

00:14:36.010 --> 00:14:39.520
And only when we're ready, would
it actually do the cutover.

00:14:39.520 --> 00:14:43.000
So this was a fantastic tool.

00:14:43.000 --> 00:14:46.090
A little bit deeper
dive on that, this

00:14:46.090 --> 00:14:47.704
is how we did it,
every single night,

00:14:47.704 --> 00:14:49.870
with minimal maintenance
window and minimal downtime

00:14:49.870 --> 00:14:52.520
for our customers.

00:14:52.520 --> 00:14:54.280
So we would update the
DNS record to point

00:14:54.280 --> 00:14:56.680
to the new Google Cloud DNS.

00:14:56.680 --> 00:14:59.680
We'd move all of the files,
we'd update the NGINX layer

00:14:59.680 --> 00:15:03.070
that we talked about,
we'd move any last pieces

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:06.920
of the database, and then
the customer would be live.

00:15:06.920 --> 00:15:10.000
We did this all with
a 5- to 15-minute

00:15:10.000 --> 00:15:13.910
maintenance window per customer
or per cluster of customers.

00:15:13.910 --> 00:15:16.460
So that was how we did
it every single night.

00:15:16.460 --> 00:15:18.704
And I believe it took us
about 2 and 1/2, 3 weeks,

00:15:18.704 --> 00:15:20.370
to get through these
nightly migrations.

00:15:23.882 --> 00:15:25.840
So I'd love to also share
some of the successes

00:15:25.840 --> 00:15:27.464
and the learnings we
had along the way.

00:15:31.430 --> 00:15:33.310
Sometimes code
just doesn't work.

00:15:33.310 --> 00:15:36.010
And, in fact, we had
over 100 Jira tickets

00:15:36.010 --> 00:15:37.937
that we had filed
before the migration,

00:15:37.937 --> 00:15:40.520
and there were some issues that
we didn't catch along the way.

00:15:40.520 --> 00:15:42.820
But I'll give you an example
of what you'd look for.

00:15:42.820 --> 00:15:46.120
Anything hard-coded IP, anything
hard-coded server names,

00:15:46.120 --> 00:15:47.590
anything hard-coded
file shares--

00:15:47.590 --> 00:15:49.506
those are things that
we would have to change.

00:15:53.990 --> 00:15:56.514
We thought that, by
using multiregion,

00:15:56.514 --> 00:15:58.180
that we would be
closer to the shoppers,

00:15:58.180 --> 00:16:00.160
and we'd be closer
to the merchants.

00:16:00.160 --> 00:16:02.590
But that didn't necessarily
work out the right way.

00:16:02.590 --> 00:16:06.310
Because of the way that
Windows likes to route traffic,

00:16:06.310 --> 00:16:07.720
and NGINX likes
to route traffic,

00:16:07.720 --> 00:16:11.500
and Linux likes to route
traffic, that kind of diversity

00:16:11.500 --> 00:16:14.680
caused saturation
at the WAN layer,

00:16:14.680 --> 00:16:16.970
and we had unpredictable
traffic patterns.

00:16:16.970 --> 00:16:18.530
And by going to
a single region--

00:16:18.530 --> 00:16:20.080
and we settled on Central--

00:16:20.080 --> 00:16:22.360
we were able to get
consistent performance

00:16:22.360 --> 00:16:23.830
and predictable performance.

00:16:23.830 --> 00:16:26.097
And then later, the team
can decide on how to tweak

00:16:26.097 --> 00:16:27.430
and tune that to make it faster.

00:16:30.820 --> 00:16:34.480
And in fact, here's one of
the tweaks that they did.

00:16:34.480 --> 00:16:39.250
So NGINX, by default, it goes
to HTTP 1.0, which is stateless.

00:16:39.250 --> 00:16:43.660
By turning on HTTP 1.1
persistent connections, once

00:16:43.660 --> 00:16:45.280
again, with no code
changes, we were

00:16:45.280 --> 00:16:49.210
able to deliver another
50% first-byte improvement

00:16:49.210 --> 00:16:52.060
on top of that 15% I
had mentioned earlier.

00:16:52.060 --> 00:16:54.670
It's a 50% improvement
on first byte--

00:16:54.670 --> 00:16:55.480
no code change.

00:16:59.120 --> 00:17:02.036
We had a challenge on
migration latency--

00:17:02.036 --> 00:17:04.160
basically moving all those
thousands of those files

00:17:04.160 --> 00:17:06.920
over-- over the years, we'd
built all these scripts

00:17:06.920 --> 00:17:09.230
and tools to be able to
shuffle customers around

00:17:09.230 --> 00:17:11.780
in our internal data
center infrastructure.

00:17:11.780 --> 00:17:13.550
And we just thought
that they would work.

00:17:13.550 --> 00:17:16.190
All of these scripts would
work if we pointed the end,

00:17:16.190 --> 00:17:19.009
the destination to cloud,
and then they didn't.

00:17:19.009 --> 00:17:20.550
And that's where
CloudEndure came in.

00:17:24.710 --> 00:17:26.690
Some other things that
were pretty remarkable

00:17:26.690 --> 00:17:28.310
happened along the way too.

00:17:28.310 --> 00:17:30.710
Because we had
training and learning

00:17:30.710 --> 00:17:34.220
and workshops at
Volusion, other teams

00:17:34.220 --> 00:17:36.740
caught a glimpse of what
the power of GCP is.

00:17:36.740 --> 00:17:39.230
And in this case,
the marketing team,

00:17:39.230 --> 00:17:41.990
they learned all
about NGINX, and they

00:17:41.990 --> 00:17:46.040
decided to use NGINX for A/B
testing, our www.volusion.com

00:17:46.040 --> 00:17:46.756
site.

00:17:46.756 --> 00:17:48.880
And there are a lot of A/B
testing tools out there,

00:17:48.880 --> 00:17:50.755
but what we're trying
to do is pretty unique.

00:17:50.755 --> 00:17:52.310
We had a rebrand effort.

00:17:52.310 --> 00:17:55.100
And sometime during
the migration,

00:17:55.100 --> 00:17:56.660
these tools came about.

00:17:56.660 --> 00:17:58.640
And we were able
to split traffic

00:17:58.640 --> 00:18:02.960
from our classic nonbranded
site to the new branded site

00:18:02.960 --> 00:18:06.020
without redirecting anyone
to a new context path.

00:18:06.020 --> 00:18:08.460
That means everybody
stayed on www.volusion.com.

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:12.890
They didn't go to /B or /A,
and it was pretty smart.

00:18:12.890 --> 00:18:15.500
This layer in between
would check the URI string,

00:18:15.500 --> 00:18:18.260
it would check query string,
it would check cookies.

00:18:18.260 --> 00:18:22.700
And it would persist that user
to that particular A/B test.

00:18:22.700 --> 00:18:25.070
So this was a great win.

00:18:25.070 --> 00:18:27.470
And the marketing
team also decided

00:18:27.470 --> 00:18:31.580
to use buckets and
Ghost to host the blog.

00:18:31.580 --> 00:18:35.524
So our previous version of the
blog was actually pretty slow.

00:18:35.524 --> 00:18:37.940
And we didn't realize it until
we realized how fast it was

00:18:37.940 --> 00:18:40.070
once we moved it over to GCP.

00:18:40.070 --> 00:18:42.260
But once we did move it
over to Google Cloud,

00:18:42.260 --> 00:18:44.920
and we took advantage
of Cloud CDN,

00:18:44.920 --> 00:18:46.760
this thing was blazing fast.

00:18:46.760 --> 00:18:49.960
And it's gone through a
few more iterations now,

00:18:49.960 --> 00:18:54.660
but it's definitely a
win to mention here.

00:18:54.660 --> 00:18:57.420
So as I mentioned,
first-byte response times

00:18:57.420 --> 00:19:01.860
have improved for our customers
by 65% with no code change.

00:19:05.310 --> 00:19:07.650
And we have been able
to save even more money

00:19:07.650 --> 00:19:09.750
than we originally forecasted.

00:19:09.750 --> 00:19:13.080
That's money that we can spend
on product development and R&amp;D.

00:19:13.080 --> 00:19:16.530
So right now, we
are saving 37% month

00:19:16.530 --> 00:19:18.320
over month on our cost-to-serve.

00:19:18.320 --> 00:19:20.289
And we feel like we're
going to keep going down

00:19:20.289 --> 00:19:21.580
that path of saving more money.

00:19:24.704 --> 00:19:26.120
And what's in it
for the customer?

00:19:26.120 --> 00:19:28.130
Here's a quote from
one of our customers.

00:19:28.130 --> 00:19:30.830
Derek operates a
sporting goods store.

00:19:30.830 --> 00:19:33.620
It's a paintball
store called ANS Gear.

00:19:33.620 --> 00:19:35.840
The quote is actually
really long here.

00:19:35.840 --> 00:19:38.681
I'm going to call attention to
the parts that you can't see,

00:19:38.681 --> 00:19:40.430
which is the fact that,
for the first time

00:19:40.430 --> 00:19:44.570
ever, he was able to run
a marketing promotion 100%

00:19:44.570 --> 00:19:48.410
on our platform without seeing
any degradation in performance

00:19:48.410 --> 00:19:50.450
of his response
time on his site.

00:19:50.450 --> 00:19:53.450
And historically,
site traffic goes up--

00:19:53.450 --> 00:19:56.030
even a half second
latency causes

00:19:56.030 --> 00:19:59.360
sales to not go up,
or maybe even dip.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:01.590
That didn't happen
on GCP at all.

00:20:01.590 --> 00:20:03.440
So this is a fantastic
win for the customer.

00:20:07.550 --> 00:20:09.800
We've got to take a
step back and celebrate

00:20:09.800 --> 00:20:12.050
sometimes, celebrate
your wins as a company.

00:20:12.050 --> 00:20:16.790
And we got a chance to do that
both publicly and internally.

00:20:16.790 --> 00:20:19.750
We got to celebrate being the
first to migrate to cloud,

00:20:19.750 --> 00:20:21.410
and we had a big
party internally

00:20:21.410 --> 00:20:22.984
to celebrate the achievement.

00:20:26.882 --> 00:20:28.590
So if you only have
to remember one thing

00:20:28.590 --> 00:20:31.320
from this presentation, it's
that, whatever decision you

00:20:31.320 --> 00:20:33.430
make, whether you
want to move to cloud,

00:20:33.430 --> 00:20:35.040
whether you're
already on the cloud,

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:37.900
you should ask yourself,
what's in it for the customer?

00:20:37.900 --> 00:20:40.220
And if you can remember
a couple of things,

00:20:40.220 --> 00:20:43.766
it's that learning and
persistence and perseverance

00:20:43.766 --> 00:20:44.640
is how you get there.

00:20:44.640 --> 00:20:46.830
Because there were so many
times that we could give up,

00:20:46.830 --> 00:20:48.420
so many times it's
easy to say, no, we're

00:20:48.420 --> 00:20:49.711
not going to do that right now.

00:20:49.711 --> 00:20:51.912
We're just going to go to
a colo and call it a day.

00:20:51.912 --> 00:20:53.370
But it's that
perseverance attitude

00:20:53.370 --> 00:20:54.620
that's going to get you there.

00:20:57.270 --> 00:20:58.670
So thank you so much.

00:20:58.670 --> 00:21:01.220
We have created an email
address for any questions.

00:21:01.220 --> 00:21:04.270
[MUSIC PLAYING]

